(1 row)

ALTER TABLE tc_t ALTER COLUMN i4 TYPE smallint;                                 -- expect ERROR
ERROR:  paimon_slot: cannot change the type of replicated column public.tc_t.i4 from integer to smallint: it narrows an integer
HINT:  PostgreSQL rewrites the table without sending the rewritten rows, so Paimon keeps the old values and reads them through its own cast. Only exact widenings are allowed (smallint->int->bigint, integer->wide-enough numeric or text, real->double precision, numeric(p,s)->numeric with no fewer integer digits or decimal places, text<->varchar, timestamp precision up). Otherwise: ADD COLUMN the new type, UPDATE it from the old one, DROP the old column, RENAME the new one -- each step replicates.
ALTER TABLE tc_t ALTER COLUMN i8 TYPE int;                                      -- expect ERROR
ERROR:  paimon_slot: cannot change the type of replicated column public.tc_t.i8 from bigint to integer: it narrows an integer
HINT:  PostgreSQL rewrites the table without sending the rewritten rows, so Paimon keeps the old values and reads them through its own cast. Only exact widenings are allowed (smallint->int->bigint, integer->wide-enough numeric or text, real->double precision, numeric(p,s)->numeric with no fewer integer digits or decimal places, text<->varchar, timestamp precision up). Otherwise: ADD COLUMN the new type, UPDATE it from the old one, DROP the old column, RENAME the new one -- each step replicates.
ALTER TABLE tc_t ALTER COLUMN i4 TYPE numeric(9,0);                             -- expect ERROR
ERROR:  paimon_slot: cannot change the type of replicated column public.tc_t.i4 from integer to numeric(9,0): the numeric has too few integer digits for every value of the integer type
HINT:  PostgreSQL rewrites the table without sending the rewritten rows, so Paimon keeps the old values and reads them through its own cast. Only exact widenings are allowed (smallint->int->bigint, integer->wide-enough numeric or text, real->double precision, numeric(p,s)->numeric with no fewer integer digits or decimal places, text<->varchar, timestamp precision up). Otherwise: ADD COLUMN the new type, UPDATE it from the old one, DROP the old column, RENAME the new one -- each step replicates.
ALTER TABLE tc_t ALTER COLUMN f8 TYPE real;                                     -- expect ERROR
ERROR:  paimon_slot: cannot change the type of replicated column public.tc_t.f8 from double precision to real: Paimon could not apply it to the rows it already holds without changing them
HINT:  PostgreSQL rewrites the table without sending the rewritten rows, so Paimon keeps the old values and reads them through its own cast. Only exact widenings are allowed (smallint->int->bigint, integer->wide-enough numeric or text, real->double precision, numeric(p,s)->numeric with no fewer integer digits or decimal places, text<->varchar, timestamp precision up). Otherwise: ADD COLUMN the new type, UPDATE it from the old one, DROP the old column, RENAME the new one -- each step replicates.
ALTER TABLE tc_t ALTER COLUMN f4 TYPE numeric(10,2);                            -- expect ERROR
ERROR:  paimon_slot: cannot change the type of replicated column public.tc_t.f4 from real to numeric(10,2): Paimon could not apply it to the rows it already holds without changing them
HINT:  PostgreSQL rewrites the table without sending the rewritten rows, so Paimon keeps the old values and reads them through its own cast. Only exact widenings are allowed (smallint->int->bigint, integer->wide-enough numeric or text, real->double precision, numeric(p,s)->numeric with no fewer integer digits or decimal places, text<->varchar, timestamp precision up). Otherwise: ADD COLUMN the new type, UPDATE it from the old one, DROP the old column, RENAME the new one -- each step replicates.
